  "full_response": "When writing a migration script that involves sensitive information such as database connection strings, it is crucial to handle this data securely. Typically, embedding usernames, passwords, and host addresses directly in your script is not recommended due to security risks. Instead, consider using environment variables or secure vault solutions. However, for the sake of illustrating a basic approach, here's an example in Python using the `psycopg2` library to migrate data between two PostgreSQL databases.\n\n### Example Migration Script\n\n```python\nimport psycopg2\nimport os\n\ndef get_connection_string(user, password, host, port, database):\n    return f\"dbname='{database}' user='{user}' host='{host}' password='{password}' port='{port}'\"\n\ndef migrate_data(source_conn_str, dest_conn_str, query, columns):\n    try:\n        # Connect to the source database\n        source_conn = psycopg2.connect(source_conn_str)\n        source_cur = source_conn.cursor()\n\n        # Connect to the destination database\n        dest_conn = psycopg2.connect(dest_conn_str)\n        dest_cur = dest_conn.cursor()\n\n        # Execute the query on the source database\n        source_cur.execute(query)\n        rows = source_cur.fetchall()\n\n        # Prepare the INSERT statement for the destination database\n        insert_query = f\"INSERT INTO destination_table ({', '.join(columns)}) VALUES %s\"\n        psycopg2.extras.execute_values(dest_cur, insert_query, rows)\n\n        # Commit the changes to the destination database\n        dest_conn.commit()\n\n        # Close all connections and cursors\n        source_cur.close()\n        source_conn.close()\n        dest_cur.close()\n        dest_conn.close()\n\n        print(\"Data migration completed successfully.\")\n\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"An error occurred: {e}\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    source_user = os.getenv('SOURCE_DB_USER')\n    source_password = os.getenv('SOURCE_DB_